(5,'742a37a570bd',1,'Neurologic anatomy','Which glial cell forms myelin in the central nervous system?','Oligodendrocyte',['Schwann cell','Microglial cell','Ependymal cell'],'Oligodendrocytes form CNS myelin. Schwann cells form myelin in the peripheral nervous system.'),
(5,'742a37a570bd',2,'Neurologic anatomy','Which finding most strongly supports an established upper motor neuron lesion?','An extensor plantar response',['Marked fasciculations','Loss of tendon reflexes','Flaccid paralysis with focal wasting'],'An extensor plantar response indicates corticospinal tract dysfunction. Established upper motor neuron lesions commonly cause spasticity and hyperreflexia.'),
(5,'742a37a570bd',2,'Neurologic anatomy','Which tract lesion classically causes internuclear ophthalmoplegia?','Medial longitudinal fasciculus',['Optic tract','Spinothalamic tract','Fasciculus gracilis'],'A medial longitudinal fasciculus lesion impairs adduction of the ipsilateral eye during horizontal gaze, often with nystagmus of the abducting eye.'),
(5,'127004871935',1,'Multiple sclerosis','Multiple sclerosis primarily affects myelin in which location?','The central nervous system',['The neuromuscular junction','Peripheral sensory nerves only','Skeletal muscle fibres'],'Multiple sclerosis is an inflammatory demyelinating disease of the CNS, including the brain, optic nerves and spinal cord.'),
(5,'127004871935',3,'Multiple sclerosis','Which CSF finding supports a diagnosis of multiple sclerosis in the appropriate clinical setting?','CSF-restricted oligoclonal IgG bands',['Very low glucose with neutrophilic pleocytosis','Malignant cells','A positive bacterial culture'],'CSF-restricted oligoclonal bands indicate intrathecal immunoglobulin production. They support the diagnosis in context but are not specific to multiple sclerosis.'),
(5,'956aff4d4434',1,'Movement disorders','Which feature is central to the clinical syndrome of parkinsonism?','Bradykinesia',['Isolated sensory loss','Flaccid areflexia','Sudden visual loss'],'Parkinsonism requires bradykinesia with rigidity or rest tremor. Bradykinesia includes slowness and decrement of repetitive movement.'),
(8,'96174aabc074',14,'General anesthesia','Ketamine primarily produces anesthesia through antagonism of which receptor?','NMDA receptor',['Muscarinic acetylcholine receptor','Dopamine D2 receptor','Histamine H1 receptor'],'Ketamine produces dissociative anesthesia primarily through noncompetitive NMDA-receptor antagonism.'),
(8,'96174aabc074',27,'Neuromuscular blockade','Which drug is a depolarizing neuromuscular blocker?','Succinylcholine',['Rocuronium','Vecuronium','Cisatracurium'],'Succinylcholine activates nicotinic receptors and produces persistent depolarization. Rocuronium, vecuronium and cisatracurium are nondepolarizing blockers.'),
(8,'96174aabc074',11,'Malignant hyperthermia','Which drug is used to treat an acute malignant hyperthermia crisis?','Dantrolene',['Naloxone','Flumazenil','Protamine'],'Dantrolene reduces abnormal skeletal-muscle calcium release. Management also requires stopping triggering agents and immediate supportive treatment.'),
(8,'96174aabc074',11,'Malignant hyperthermia','Which combination can trigger malignant hyperthermia in a susceptible patient?','Volatile anesthetic agents and succinylcholine',['Propofol and fentanyl','Lidocaine and bupivacaine','Nitrous oxide and midazolam'],'Potent volatile anesthetics and succinylcholine are established malignant-hyperthermia triggers in susceptible individuals.'),
(4,'ffb355413118',1,'Primary health care','In which year was the Declaration of Alma-Ata adopted?','1978',['1948','1986','2000'],'The 1978 Declaration of Alma-Ata identified primary health care as essential to achieving health for all.'),
(4,'ffb355413118',1,'Primary health care','Which principle of primary health care means involving people in planning and delivering their care?','Community participation',['Exclusive specialist control','Hospital centralization','Commercial advertising'],'Primary health care involves individuals and communities in decisions about services and health priorities.'),
(4,'ffb355413118',1,'Primary health care','Which combination best reflects the scope of comprehensive primary health care?','Promotion, prevention, treatment and rehabilitation',['Emergency surgery only','Vaccination only','Treatment of established disease only'],'Comprehensive primary health care includes promotion, prevention, treatment and rehabilitation rather than a single type of service.'),
(4,'51f8e61bdaf0',2,'Communication skills','Which is an example of nonverbal communication?','Body posture and gestures',['The words chosen','Written instructions','The literal content of a question'],'Posture, gestures, facial expression and interpersonal distance convey nonverbal messages.'),
(4,'51f8e61bdaf0',2,'Communication skills','Which behaviour is a barrier to effective verbal communication?','Using unexplained medical jargon',['Using clear language','Organizing information','Checking understanding'],'Unexplained jargon can obscure meaning. Use plain language and check the patient’s understanding.'),
(4,'51f8e61bdaf0',2,'Communication skills','Which is NOT a supportive verbal listening technique?','Blaming the patient',['Paraphrasing','Summarizing','Reflecting feelings'],'Blame discourages disclosure and damages trust. Paraphrasing, summarizing and reflecting feelings support active listening.'),
(4,'4182abe4bcac',2,'Breaking bad news','In SPIKES, which step asks how much information the patient wants?','Invitation',['Perception','Knowledge','Strategy and summary'],'Invitation establishes the patient’s preference for the amount and detail of information.'),
(4,'4182abe4bcac',2,'Breaking bad news','In SPIKES, a warning shot before disclosing a serious diagnosis belongs to which step?','Knowledge',['Setting','Invitation','Strategy and summary'],'During Knowledge, a brief warning prepares the patient before clear, paced disclosure.'),
(4,'4182abe4bcac',2,'Breaking bad news','What does the P in SPIKES stand for?','Perception',['Prognosis','Prescription','Prevention'],'Perception explores what the patient already understands about the illness before new information is given.'),
(4,'4182abe4bcac',2,'Breaking bad news','Which action best illustrates the final S in SPIKES?','Agreeing on a plan and summarizing the discussion',['Withholding the diagnosis','Giving all information at once','Ignoring emotional responses'],'Strategy and summary consolidate understanding and agree on the next steps with the patient.'),
(4,'e912a84e8b3e',2,'Counseling','Which practice undermines effective counseling?','Rushing through the discussion',['Using a private setting','Maintaining confidentiality','Using clear language'],'Counseling requires sufficient time, privacy, a nonjudgmental approach and information the patient can understand.'),
(4,'e912a84e8b3e',2,'Counseling','Encouraging a sedentary patient to start regular exercise is an example of which behavioural goal?','Adding a beneficial behaviour',['Eliminating an addictive behaviour','Eliminating a nonaddictive behaviour','Changing the diagnosis'],'Starting exercise adds a health-promoting behaviour rather than removing an existing behaviour.'),
(4,'e912a84e8b3e',2,'Counseling','Which approach best supports achievable behaviour change?','Agreeing on a small number of realistic goals',['Changing every habit immediately','Giving orders without discussion','Avoiding follow-up'],'Patient-centred goals should be realistic, limited in number and supported by a practical plan and follow-up.'),
(4,'c27650c287bb',2,'Doctor–patient relationship','Which approach is inconsistent with patient-centred care?','An authoritarian approach that suppresses the patient’s views',['Open-ended questions','Exploring psychosocial concerns','Shared decision-making'],'Patient-centred care explores the patient’s concerns, values and circumstances and involves them in decisions.'),
(4,'029e4dc10fb8',2,'Difficult consultations','Which physician factor can contribute to a difficult consultation?','Fatigue',['A patient’s chronic pain','A patient’s unmet expectations','A patient’s functional impairment'],'Fatigue can reduce patience and communication quality. Difficult encounters can reflect clinician, patient and situational factors.'),
(4,'d4d4e70813bf',2,'Evidence-based medicine','Participants are randomly allocated to nicotine inhalers or nicotine patches. What is the study design?','Randomized controlled trial',['Case report','Cross-sectional study','Systematic review'],'Random allocation to intervention groups characterizes a randomized controlled trial.'),
